Dear OEIS editors,

Please consider the attached b084945.txt as a replacement b-file for A084945
(Decimal expansion of Golomb-Dickman constant). OEIS currently serves a
synthesized b-file for n = 0..101; the attached file gives n = 0..74999.

Suggested link text for the Links section:

  Konstantin Pozdnyakov, <a href="b084945.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n=0..74999</a>
  (terms 0..101 previously in OEIS; terms 102..74999 from this submission)

Suggested comment:

  Terms 0..74999 are the first 75,000 decimal digits of the Golomb-Dickman
  constant, certified by interval arithmetic (Arb): the enclosing interval
  cannot cross a decimal boundary, so the truncation is proved.

The 75,000 digits come from a published, archived computation:

  Version DOI:  https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24567561
  Zenodo record: https://zenodo.org/records/24567561
  GitHub release: https://github.com/fix27/golomb-dickman-constant/releases/tag/v75000
  Certificate/code/checkers: https://github.com/fix27/golomb-dickman-constant/tree/main/75000

The certificate consists of integers Lambda_- < Lambda_+ and a scale S = 249341
such that lambda is in [Lambda_-/2^S, Lambda_+/2^S] and

    floor(10^75000 * Lambda_- / 2^S)
  = floor(10^75000 * Lambda_+/2^S),

so the first 75,000 digits are proved by truncation, not by floating-point
rounding. The interval width is 5.8205594787 * 10^-75043, leaving about
42.235 guard digits beyond the last certified place. The producing code,
the 4,767 checkpoint chunks, the plan (meta_v13.json, format gd-checkpoint-v13)
and a dependency-free verifier are in the 75000/ directory of the repository.
The first 102 digits agree with the current OEIS b-file. An earlier
50,000-digit certificate is preserved in `50000/`, but its separate digits
file is no longer distributed, so no `--compare` step is included here.

Verification needs only Python 3.11+ and the standard library:

  python verification/verify_certificate.py \
      --cert 75000/golomb_dickman_75000_certified.txt \
      --digits-file golomb_dickman_75000_digits.txt \
      --sha256-manifest 75000/SHA256SUMS.txt \
      --bfile submission/b084945.txt \
      --sha256 b084945.txt=05584c9d3f0afccbfb9a1d8c8f28177a33a30be3ec880dc6ffe481a136b19241

It prints "OK: 75000 digits certified (interval arithmetic)" and
"SHA-256: MATCH (manifest)". An independent mpmath quadrature with tanh-sinh
and Gauss-Legendre gave 320 matching digits. For transparency: a full
independent Arb re-audit of all 476,648 nodes was NOT run (2,000 nodes were
checked), and normality of lambda is open. This is an extension of a
published computation, not a world-record or priority claim.

SHA-256 (canonical LF bytes, no BOM):

  05584c9d3f0afccbfb9a1d8c8f28177a33a30be3ec880dc6ffe481a136b19241  b084945.txt
  b177fc551c8a9f950e037fdc92ed72fb9219eb11d81999410967f8013e6df534  golomb_dickman_75000_certified.txt
  0c5f2b212eb5fd25b1def672226f65f46b97d44c5e371c5db10ab1cef9b468aa  golomb_dickman_75000_digits.txt

The b-file is plain ASCII with LF line endings, 75,000 terms, indices
0..74999, no comment lines, one trailing blank line, 588,891 bytes,
75,001 lines in total. The data lines contain one decimal digit each, so they
are well below any line-length limit, and the whole file is far below the
10 MB upload limit. If 75,000 terms is considered too long,
I can supply a truncated version (10,000 / 20,000 / 50,000 terms) with a new
checksum on request; the prefix is unchanged.

